/*!
@brief TCP sockets: a listener that takes connections, and the connection itself.

Two classes over one native handle. `TCPListener` waits for clients,
`TCPStream` is a client once it has arrived, and the role is decided by the
class holding the handle rather than by the handle itself.

Both are built in two steps, which is what keeps them configurable: the
constructor opens the socket, and a second call commits it to the network.
Between the two the descriptor exists and is still untouched by the system,
which is the only moment a kernel honors options such as binding to an
interface, IPV6_V6ONLY or buffer sizes. Reach them through `fileno`.

When none of that is needed, `net.listen` does both steps in one call.

Every operation that has to wait parks the fiber on the event loop instead of
blocking its scheduler thread, so a server built on these classes serves many
connections from one thread without threads of its own.
*/

import TCPHandle, TCP_REUSEADDR from "::orbit::net"

import OSError from "error"
import Readable, Writeable from "io/traits"
import Socket from "net/traits"

/**
@brief A TCP socket that accepts incoming connections.

Built in two steps. `new TCPListener(addr)` opens a socket of the address's
family and remembers where it is meant to go; `listen()` binds it there and
starts accepting. Between the two the socket is open and unbound, which is the
moment for any option a kernel only honors before a bind.

The descriptor exists from construction, so the `defer` that closes it belongs
right after `new`, not after `listen`.

@see TCPStream, Socket

@example
    l := new TCPListener(net.parse("127.0.0.1", 0))
    defer l.close()

    l.listen(backlog=128)
    io.print(l.local_addr().unpack())    // ("127.0.0.1", 49182, @INET)

    loop {
        conn := l.accept()
        io.print(conn.local_addr().unpack())
        conn.close()
    }
*/
pub class TCPListener impl Socket {
    var handle
    var l_addr

    # @OPEN -> @LISTENING -> @CLOSED, in that order and no other. It tells the
    # address we asked for apart from the one the system settled on, and keeps
    # accept off a socket that is not accepting. A closed listener is left to
    # the handle, which reports the bad descriptor better than we could.
    var state = @OPEN

    /**
    @brief Open a socket for the family of `addr`, to listen there later.

    Nothing reaches the network yet: the address is only read for its family,
    and is handed to the system by `listen`. Building a listener therefore
    never blocks and never fails for a reason outside this process, such as an
    address already in use, which is what `listen` reports instead.

    @param addr The address to listen on, as a Sockaddr. Use port 0 to let the
                system choose one, and read it back from `local_addr` once
                `listen` has run.

    @panic TypeError  When `addr` is not a Sockaddr.
    @panic ValueError When `addr` belongs to a family TCP does not speak.
    @panic OSError    When the socket cannot be opened, out of descriptors for
                      instance.

    @example
        new TCPListener(net.parse("0.0.0.0", 8080))     // every interface
        new TCPListener(net.parse("127.0.0.1", 0))      // loopback, any port
    */
    pub init(addr) {
        host, port, family := addr.unpack()

        self.handle = TCPHandle.open(family)

        self.l_addr = addr
    }

    /**
    @brief Take the next connection that has arrived.

    The fiber is parked until a client connects, so the scheduler thread stays
    free for other fibers; only this fiber waits. A connection already waiting
    is taken inside the call and nothing is parked at all.

    The listener goes on accepting afterwards, and after a failure too, so a
    server can report the reason and ask for the next connection.

    @param timeout=0 Milliseconds to wait for a connection, 0 to wait for as
                     long as it takes.

    @return A TCPStream for the connection, ready to read and write.

    @panic OSError  When the listener is not listening yet, when it is closed,
                    or when no connection arrived within `timeout`. Closing the
                    listener from another fiber also ends a waiting accept this
                    way.

    @see listen, close

    @example
        conn := l.accept()               // waits as long as it takes
        conn := l.accept(timeout=5000)   // OSError after five seconds
    */
    pub func accept(timeout=0) {
        if self.state == @OPEN {
            panic OSError("listener is not listening, call listen() first")
        }

        remote := self.handle.accept(timeout=timeout)

        return new TCPStream(remote)
    }

    /**
    @brief Close the listener and stop accepting.

    Returns at once. An accept another fiber is waiting on is cancelled and
    raises there, which is how a server is asked to stop. Idempotent, so
    closing a listener that is already closed does nothing, and every other
    method raises on one.

    @panic OSError When the event loop could not take the close. The listener
                   is left open in that case, so the call can be tried again.

    @see accept
    */
    pub func close() {
        self.handle.close()

        self.state = @CLOSED
    }

    /**
    @brief Return the descriptor the system knows this listener by.

    The window between `new` and `listen` is where this is worth using: a
    socket that is open and not yet bound is the only one a kernel will accept
    certain options on.

    @return The descriptor, as the number the system knows it by.

    @panic OSError When the listener is closed.

    @see close, listen

    @example
        l := new TCPListener(net.parse("0.0.0.0", 8080))
        set_an_option(l.fileno())        // before listen, while it is unbound
        l.listen()
    */
    pub func fileno() {
        return self.handle.fileno()
    }

    /**
    @brief Bind to the address given at construction and start accepting.

    Both steps happen here, because there is nothing useful to do between them
    and folding the two removes a state in which the listener is bound but deaf.
    The system answers at once, so nothing is parked.

    This is where the failures that depend on the rest of the machine show up:
    the address is in use, the port is privileged, the address is not one of
    this host's.

    @param backlog=128      Connections the kernel may hold before it starts
                            refusing them.
    @param reuse_addr=true  Allow binding an address still held by the
                            connections of a previous run, which is what lets a
                            server restart at once instead of waiting out the
                            old connections.

    @panic ValueError When `backlog` is negative or larger than the system
                      allows.
    @panic OSError    When the listener is closed, when it is already
                      listening, or when the address cannot be bound.

    @see accept, local_addr

    @example
        l.listen()                                   // 128 pending, address reused
        l.listen(backlog=512, reuse_addr=false)
    */
    pub func listen(backlog=128, reuse_addr=true) {
        if self.state == @LISTENING {
            panic OSError("listener is already listening")
        }

        flags := 0
        if reuse_addr {
            flags = TCP_REUSEADDR
        }

        self.handle.bind(self.l_addr, flags=flags)
        self.handle.listen(backlog=backlog)

        self.state = @LISTENING
    }

    /**
    @brief Return the address the listener occupies.

    Before `listen` this is the address it was built for, since the system has
    not been told about it yet. Afterwards it is what the system actually did,
    which is the difference that matters when the port was 0: only the second
    answer names the port that was chosen.

    @return The local address, as a Sockaddr.

    @panic OSError  When the listener is closed.

    @see listen

    @example
        l := new TCPListener(net.parse("127.0.0.1", 0))
        io.print(l.local_addr().unpack())    // ("127.0.0.1", 0, @INET)
        l.listen()
        io.print(l.local_addr().unpack())    // ("127.0.0.1", 49182, @INET)
    */
    pub func local_addr() {
        if self.state == @OPEN {
            return self.l_addr
        }

        return self.handle.local_addr()
    }
}

/**
@brief One established TCP connection.

Reads and writes bytes over a connection somebody else opened: `accept` on a
listener hands one over. It implements `Readable` and `Writeable`, so it drops
straight into anything built on those traits, `bufio.BufferedReader` above all.

The same transfers are spelled twice. `read`, `readinto` and `write` are the
trait vocabulary and wait for as long as it takes; `recv`, `recv_into` and
`send` are the same operations with a deadline, for code that talks to the
network knowingly and does not want to wait forever.

Reads are short by design: one read returns what has arrived, not what was
asked for, and an empty result means the peer is done sending. Writes are not
short: `write` returns once every byte has left, or raises.

@see TCPListener, Socket, Readable, Writeable

@example
    conn := l.accept()
    defer conn.close()

    line := new bufio.BufferedReader(conn).readline(-1)
    conn.write(b"echo: " + line)
*/
pub class TCPStream impl Socket + Readable + Writeable {
    var handle

    /**
    @brief Wrap a connected handle.

    Not the usual way in: a stream comes from `TCPListener.accept`, which
    builds it. Use this directly only with a handle prepared by hand through
    the native `::orbit::net` module.

    @param handle A connected TCPHandle, which the stream owns from here on.
    */
    pub init(handle) {
        self.handle = handle
    }

    /**
    @brief Close the connection.

    Returns at once, and for the peer this is the end of the connection in both
    directions. Reads and writes another fiber is waiting on are cancelled and
    raise there. Idempotent, so closing a stream that is already closed does
    nothing, and every other method raises on one.

    @panic OSError When the event loop could not take the close. The stream is
                   left open in that case, so the call can be tried again.
    */
    pub func close() {
        self.handle.close()
    }

    /**
    @brief Return the descriptor the system knows this connection by.

    @return The descriptor, as the number the system knows it by.

    @panic OSError When the stream is closed.

    @see close
    */
    pub func fileno() {
        return self.handle.fileno()
    }

    /**
    @brief Do nothing, successfully.

    A connection has no buffer of its own: `write` has already handed every
    byte to the system by the time it returns. The method is here because
    `Writeable` asks for it, so that a stream can stand in for a sink that does
    buffer.

    @see write
    */
    pub func flush() {
        # Do nothing
        return nil
    }

    /**
    @brief Return the local address of this end of the connection.

    @return The local address, as a Sockaddr.

    @panic OSError  When the stream is closed.
    */
    pub func local_addr() {
        return self.handle.local_addr()
    }

    /**
    @brief Read up to `length` bytes, waiting for as long as it takes.

    Returns what has arrived rather than what was asked for: a shorter result
    is the normal case, not a truncated one, and getting an exact number of
    bytes is `bufio.BufferedReader`'s job. An empty result means the peer has
    stopped sending, which is a value and not an error.

    @param length Most bytes to return. 0 returns an empty result without
                  touching the connection.

    @return A new Bytes with what arrived, empty at end of stream.

    @panic ValueError When `length` is negative.
    @panic OSError    When the stream is closed, or the connection fails.

    @see recv, readinto

    @example
        data := conn.read(4096)
        if data.length() == 0 {          // the peer is done sending
            conn.close()
        }
    */
    pub func read(length) {
        return self.handle.recv(length)
    }

    /**
    @brief Read into a buffer you own, waiting for as long as it takes.

    The same read as `read`, without the allocation: what arrives is written
    into `buffer` and only the count comes back. The buffer keeps its own
    length, so the bytes past the count are whatever was there before.

    The buffer cannot be resized while the read is in flight; another fiber
    trying to grow it raises rather than move it under the system.

    @param buffer Destination Bytes, which must hold `offset + length` bytes.
    @param offset Where in `buffer` to start writing.
    @param length Most bytes to read. 0 returns 0 without touching the
                  connection.

    @return The number of bytes written into `buffer`, 0 at end of stream.

    @panic ValueError When `offset` or `length` is negative, or the range falls
                      outside `buffer`.
    @panic OSError    When the stream is closed, or the connection fails.

    @see recv_into, read

    @example
        buf := Bytes(len=4096)
        n := conn.readinto(buf, 0, 4096)
    */
    pub func readinto(buffer, offset, length) {
        return self.handle.recv_into(buffer, offset, length)
    }

    /**
    @brief Read up to `length` bytes, giving up after `timeout`.

    `read` with a deadline, and the same short-read and end-of-stream rules.
    A deadline that arrives is a failure, not an empty result: waiting in vain
    and hearing that the peer is done are different answers and stay different.

    @param length    Most bytes to return. 0 returns an empty result without
                     touching the connection.
    @param timeout=0 Milliseconds to wait for the first byte, 0 to wait for as
                     long as it takes.

    @return A new Bytes with what arrived, empty at end of stream.

    @panic ValueError When `length` or `timeout` is negative.
    @panic OSError    When the stream is closed, when nothing arrived within
                      `timeout`, or when the connection fails.

    @see read, recv_into, send

    @example
        conn.recv(4096)                 // waits as long as it takes
        conn.recv(4096, timeout=2000)   // OSError after two seconds
    */
    pub func recv(length, timeout=0) {
        return self.handle.recv(length, timeout=timeout)
    }

    /**
    @brief Read into a buffer you own, giving up after `timeout`.

    `readinto` with a deadline. Everything else, the count, the untouched tail
    of the buffer, the 0 at end of stream, is the same.

    @param buffer    Destination Bytes, which must hold `offset + length` bytes.
    @param offset    Where in `buffer` to start writing.
    @param length    Most bytes to read. 0 returns 0 without touching the
                     connection.
    @param timeout=0 Milliseconds to wait for the first byte, 0 to wait for as
                     long as it takes.

    @return The number of bytes written into `buffer`, 0 at end of stream.

    @panic ValueError When `offset`, `length` or `timeout` is negative, or the
                      range falls outside `buffer`.
    @panic OSError    When the stream is closed, when nothing arrived within
                      `timeout`, or when the connection fails.

    @see readinto, recv

    @example
        buf := Bytes(len=4096)
        n := conn.recv_into(buf, 0, 4096, timeout=2000)
    */
    pub func recv_into(buffer, offset, length, timeout=0) {
        return self.handle.recv_into(buffer, offset, length, timeout=timeout)
    }

    /**
    @brief Send every byte of `buffer`.

    All of it or none of the outcome: the call returns once the last byte has
    left, and otherwise raises. A partial send is not something the caller has
    to handle, so there is no loop to write around this.

    Leaving does not mean arriving. The bytes have been handed to the system,
    which will deliver them or, later, tell a read that the connection broke.

    A deadline that arrives raises like any other failure, and how much of the
    buffer had already gone out is not reported, so a stream cannot be picked
    up again after one: close it.

    @param buffer    The bytes to send, as Bytes or a String.
    @param timeout=0 Milliseconds to wait for the whole buffer to leave, 0 to
                     wait for as long as it takes.

    @return The number of bytes sent, which is the whole buffer.

    @panic ValueError When `timeout` is negative.
    @panic OSError    When the stream is closed, when the buffer had not left
                      within `timeout`, or when the connection fails before
                      every byte could be sent.

    @see write, recv

    @example
        conn.send(b"pong")                 // 4
        conn.send(payload, timeout=5000)
    */
    pub func send(buffer, timeout=0) {
        return self.handle.send(buffer, timeout=timeout)
    }

    /**
    @brief Send every byte of `buffer`.

    The `Writeable` spelling of `send`, and the same operation, minus the
    deadline that the trait has no room for: use this one in code that works
    with any sink, and `send` when there has to be a timeout.

    @param buffer The bytes to send, as Bytes or a String.

    @return The number of bytes sent, which is the whole buffer.

    @panic OSError When the stream is closed, or the connection fails before
                   every byte could be sent.

    @see send, flush
    */
    pub func write(buffer) {
        return self.handle.send(buffer)
    }
}
